      "side": 0,
      "message": "Thanks for the tip to just `return False` instead of keeping a variable around.\n\nI\u0027m not sure I see the benefit of splitting this function into two though. `shouldDisableMixedBuilds` is a less descriptive name for this particular function than `allSanitizersSupportedByBazel`, and IMO `checkSanitizers` and `shouldDisableMixedBuilds` are doing the same thing (checking for unsupported sanitizers) and so should be one implementation.\n\n----\n\nAbout this:\n```\nfunc shouldDisableMixedBuild(c *Module) {\n  return (isUbsanEnabled(c) \u0026\u0026 !c.MinimalRuntimeNeeded()) \u0026\u0026\n    // This might be more maintainable. For any newly supported\n    // sanitizer in mixed build, we can add here:\n    // !isFooSanitizerEnabled(c)\n}\n```\nI do agree that this reads easier, but I think we need to use the `for` loop so that if new sanitizers are added we still account for them and we don\u0027t have to keep this function in sync with the Soong sanitizers list.",
